\{"kind":"table","sheet":"Table 2","address":"A3:G10","rows":8,"cols":7,"values":[["Study","Cohort/Model","Microbial Focus","Immune Metrics","Mechanism/Findings","Clinical Impact","Limitations"],["Taur et al. [12]","Humans (allo-HSCT, n = 80)","Alpha diversity via inverse Simpson index","Stem cell source, infection, and microbial diversity","Increased OS associated with higher relative abundance of Lachnospiraceae and Actinomycetaceae.\\n\\nDomination by Enterococcus, Streptococcus, Enterobacteriaceae, and Lactobacillus spp. linked to poor outcomes.","Replenishment of commensal bacterial populations may improve immune reconstitution and outcomes post-HSCT.","Causal relationships unclear; control interventional studies needed."],["Iwamura et al. [95]","Murine model (NOD1-/-, SPF, GF)","Microbiota in the maintenance of steady-state hematopoiesis","NOD1 and NOD2 ligands","NOD1 ligand administration restores hematopoietic precursor pools in germ-free mice to levels seen in specific pathogen-free animals.","Suggests NOD1 ligands as potential therapeutic agents to restore hematopoiesis post-HSCT.","Requires further studies to find the interaction between immune pathways."],["Staffas et al. [96]","Murine model (antibiotic-treated allo-HSCT)","Antibiotics, sucrose metabolism","Granulocyte/lymphocyte recovery","Depletion of intestinal microbiota impairs dietary energy uptake and visceral fat store.\\n\\nCaloric supplementation via sucrose restored granulopoiesis post-allo-HSCT.","Suggests postbiotics (e.g., sucrose) as potential therapies for neutrophil recovery post-HSCT.","Preclinical model; relevance to human immune reconstitution requires further validation."],["Peled et al. [6]","Humans (allo-HSCT, n = 1,362)","Loss of microbial diversity and domination of single taxa","OS, neutrophil engraftment, GVHD","Higher diversity of intestinal microbiota at the time of neutrophil engraftment was associated with increased OS.\\n\\nAbundance of Enterococcus and Streptococcus spp. linked to increased GVHD risk.","Identifies microbial diversity as a biomarker for post-HSCT outcomes.","Retrospective design; heterogeneity in conditioning regimens."],["Schluter et al. [42]","Humans (allo-HSCT, n = 841; auto-FMT, n = 24)","Microbial community dynamics","Daily counts of neutrophils, lymphocytes, and monocytes","Higher counts of WBCs in individuals receiving auto-FMT in the first 100 days after neutrophil engraftment.\\n\\nEnriched microbiota with Faecalibacterium, Ruminococcus 2, Akkermansia accelerates immune reconstitution.","Identifies specific taxa that predict immune kinetics during the engraftment phase post-HSCT.","Lacks detailed information on lymphocyte subtypes and other immune cell populations."],["Miltiadous et al. [97]","Humans (allo-HSCT, n = 894)","Early post-HSCT microbiome (d7-21)","Day 100 CD4+ / CD8+ T cells, B cells, NK cells","Increased abundance of Staphylococcus associated with poor CD4 T-cell recovery.\\n\\nHighlights Erysipelotrichaceae as beneficial for immune reconstitution.","Identifies key microbiome taxa associated with immune recovery post-HSCT.","Observational study; heterogeneity in GVHD prophylaxis regimens."],["Thiele Orberg et al. [53]","Humans (allo-HSCT, n = 78)","Bacteria and bacteriophage consortia","SCFA biosynthesis pathways, survival outcomes","High SCFA-producing pathways in bacteria/phages associated with improved survival and reduced relapse post-HSCT.","Supports the development of synthetic metabolite formulations as adjunctive therapies post-allo-HSCT.","Requires validation in larger cohorts."]]\}\
